ng the crowd a criminal  investigation is underway scott is  promising refunds for all ticket holders  and says he will also cover funeral  costs  a grim new milestone in british columbia  that province is on track for its  deadliest year from overdoses of toxic  illicit drugs the bc coroner's service  says more than 1500 people died in the  first nine months of this year that's  the highest number ever recorded in that  time period it's a 24 increase from  2020. in september 152 deaths from  illicit drugs were recorded the 12th  month in a row with more than 150 deaths  last week bc became the first province  to officially request a federal  exemption to decriminalize the  possession of hard drugs  montreal canadians goalie carey price  has gone public with his treatment for  substance abuse price released a  statement saying he checked himself into  a residential treatment facility last  month on instagram the 34 year old  father of three says i am working  through years of neglecting my own  mental health which will take some time  to repair all i can do it is take it day  by day with that comes some uncertainty  with when i will return to play  price rejoined his teammates off the ice  today for the first time since october  seventh  ahead is belarus exploiting thousands of  desperate migrants as political pawns  [Music]  a grim situation is unfolding on the  border between poland and belarus the  president of belarus alexander  lukashenka is being accused of  exploiting migrants for political  purposes he's accused of using them as  pawns encouraging them to come to  belarus and promising they can enter  europe instead they're trapped in  freezing weather on the polish border  with no shelter or food crystal  gamansing reports  here on the border of poland and belarus  hundreds of desperate people are  stranded in the freezing cold where are  you going to poland yes  yes germany  why you go to germany  the country the good the good country  belarusian officials are accused of  luring migrants from the middle east and  africa with the promise of easy access  to europe the eu says what's happening  under president alexander lukashenko is  gangster-style behavior how lukashenko  regime  conducts this operation this very very  inhuman unacceptable and actually  illegal  operation is that they reach out to  potential clients to these potential  travelers through seemingly official  channels  between june and august the european  union followed by canada the uk and the  us imposed sanctions on lukashenko and  belarus for human rights abuses  belarus is accused of weaponizing  migrants in retaliation sending  thousands to the border to provoke  conflict  lukashenko however insists belarus is  the victim of an intimidation campaign  european commissioner president ursula  van der lion has threatened more  sanctions and warned third-party  countries involved could also be  included the president demanded very  clear with the statement that belarus  stops the instrumentalization of  migrants for political purposes which is  putting people's lives at risk  there are reports several migrants have  died but information is limited  journalists are not permitted on the  polish front lines with thousands of  soldiers guarding the crossing and  blockades in place  some longing to make it into europe are  heading to neighboring latvia and  lithuania  but soldiers there are also mobilizing  along border points with belarus  crystal gomencing global news  london  100 years of the poppy next how it  became such an enduring symbol  the poppy is the flower of remembrance  day and has been for so long we rarely  stop to think about why this year is the  100th anniversary of the poppy being  adopted as a visible way to honor the  fallen and raise money for veterans mike  derlay explains how the delicate crimson  flower became such an enduring symbol  how could anything grow in the ballast  guard fields of the first world war in  every direction mud and disease yet  amidst so much death life in the form of  a brilliant red poppy caught the eye of  john mccrae which he immortalized in the  poem in flanders fields and it really  contrasts with the you know the life and  the reality of what what it was like on  the western front in world war one the  poem became an inspiration the war  office in britain began using it as a  recruiting tool but it was a pair of  women on either side of the atlantic who  saw in the poppy the potential for  charity  in georgia moyna michael is known as the  founder of the memorial poppy for  convincing the american legion in 1920  to adopt it as its emblem of remembrance  but it was a french woman named anagera  who started it all first to raise funds  for children who were orphaned during  the war and then as the symbol for  veterans we know today  so having this the poppy um transcend  uh and become this vehicle of funding to  repair these men psychologically and  otherwise is an absolutely wonderful  thing and that certainly does get its  roots from garen crossing the atlantic  garden has been described as a natural  networker after seeing the success of  her charity back home in 1921 that poppy  lady from france convinced canadian  veterans in port arthur ontario which is  now thunder bay to adopt the poppy as  its symbol  today the legion receives over 20  million dollars a year in donations  that's become essential for veterans  programs and this being the 100th  anniversary the legion not only  recreated the 1921 poppy it's entered  the world of limited digital art with an  nft called the immortal poppy  essentially it is a digital poppy what  we did is we took an original genuine  poppy from flanders fields in belgium an  artist recreated that poppy in digital  format and now it exists in 100 copies  the names of one hundred and eighteen  thousand fallen canadians are printed on  the pedals a century of sacrifice and  charity intertwined  microlight global news toronto  global news will have live coverage of  the remembrance day ceremony in ottawa  our coverage starts at 10 30 a.m eastern  7 30 pacific it will also be streaming  on our website  globalnews.ca on november 11th that is  global national for this tuesday i'm  donna friesen we leave you tonight with  this cascade of handmade poppies draping  the niagara on the lake museum in  ontario it was put together by dozens of  volunteers from the community who spent  hours crocheting and knitting nearly 3  500 flowers to honor the fallen ahead of  remembrance day  thanks for watching hope to see you here  again tomorrow take care of yourselves  bye-bye  [Music]
 

